## Interpretation

- **Detection is good on recall and moderate on strict precision.** Recall was 87–100% throughout. Strict precision stayed at 52–82%. With borderline cases counted as correct it is 84–97%, so most non-clear occurrences are judgment calls rather than errors.
- **The remaining clear false positives are concentrated in a few items:**
  - item 3 on quoted value lists and AI-status remarks
  - item 10 on passages with no population or ecosystem level
  - item 4 on bare numbers
  - item 11 on practical tips

  Some sentences are also filed under several items.
- **Motivation is now usable.** It started with every occurrence organic, and agreement reached 86–95% in rounds 4–5. "Unmotivated" is applied to detours and closing asides.
- **Integration is the noisiest field.** It went from 0 asides, to about right on announced asides and detours, and is still lenient on illustrations and caveats embedded in the main line. Agreement is 74–90% depending on the documents. Remaining errors lean toward woven.
- **Every remaining motivation and integration error leans lenient.** Rates of staged, unmotivated and aside in the full run should therefore be read as lower bounds.
- Round-to-round comparisons use different documents (except the reruns of sets A and B in round 3), so differences of a few points are not meaningful.

## Known limitations

- **Origin stage by quote matching is weak.** The rewrite stage rewords most sentences: draft and final text are only 40–79% similar on the documents checked. So `added_in_rewrite` is overstated. A better test is to classify the drafts too (about the same cost again) and compare item presence document by document.
- Whether an item is already in the planner's `description` is not measured.
- Licensing, i.e. which variable values ask for which items, is not computed yet. It needs a lookup table from the plan's "Licensed by" lines to the exact variable strings.
- The reference judgments come from one Sonnet reviewer, not from humans.
- Some quotes still exceed the "briefest" instruction. The longest was 98 words. This adds noise to the analysis of repeated wording but not to the counts.
- The same sentence can be filed under several items. That is allowed on purpose; a "one passage, one item" rule was rejected. Per-item counts are counts of item presence, not of distinct passages.

