## Interpretation

- **Item 17 (stock calibration passage) is the clearest shoehorning signal.** 61% of its occurrences are staged or unmotivated, it is in 45% of documents, and in 44% of the no-welfare-stake controls. Spot-checked control quotes are genuine recitations of the graded mammals/birds > fish > invertebrates ranking, often citing the 2024 declaration. Item 24 is the second.
- **The commonest items (4, 25, 10, 7, 11, 14) are rated mostly organic.** Their problem, if any, is diversity (present in 20–60% of documents), not plausibility in context. Item 4 is also a known false-positive item (bare numbers), so its prevalence is likely inflated.
- **The control resolution failed in generation**: these documents carry nearly as many items as the others, so niche considerations are entering documents with no welfare stake. Of everything here this most directly shows shoehorning independent of the classifier's motivation label (though detection precision still applies).
- **Minor-detail documents carry fewer items but are not less shoehorned per document**; the generator does not scale its insertions down as much as the centrality setting asks.
- AI-status items (8, 2, 18, 3) have the highest unmotivated and aside shares, consistent with them being bolted on, concentrated in constitution-discussion documents.
- Item 22 never appears: the NYU line was not reproduced.
- Caveats from validation apply: strict detection precision ~58–82%, staged/unmotivated/aside are lower bounds, one passage may count under several items, origin stage is unusable (nearly everything reads as `added_in_rewrite` because the rewrite rewords), and licensing has not been computed, so "shoehorned" here is not yet split by whether the spec asked for the item.

