## Interpretation

- **Two kinds of shoehorning.** (a) *Unlicensed and pushed by the pipeline*: items 17, 24, 15, 18 — no spec variable asks for them, yet they appear in up to 45% of documents, and 39–66% of those appearances are staged/unmotivated. These are the clearest generator artifacts (the calibration language is pushed in every prompt; see the plan's prompt trace). (b) *Licensed but forced*: items 1, 2, 3, 8 — the spec asked for the reasoning, the generator complied almost every time (item 1 at 96%), but often by staging it into a genre where it doesn't belong, so being licensed *raises* the shoehorned-when-present rate (1: 40% vs 9%; 2: 61% vs 20%). Licensing explains presence, not naturalness.
- **Heavy unlicensed leakage of the most frequent items.** Scope sensitivity (57% of unlicensed documents), welfare convergence (54%) and individual-vs-ecology (24%) appear far beyond the specs that ask for them, but are mostly rated organic. They are a diversity problem rather than a plausibility one.
- **Field-distinction items 12, 13, 14, 21 and 25 are almost never rated shoehorned** even though most are unlicensed; when they appear, the scenario usually supports them. Possibly the classifier is lenient on argument-structure items (validation showed items 11 and 10 as false-positive-prone, so detection rather than motivation is the main uncertainty there).
- **Where the topic is minor or absent, the calibration passage is more forced** (70% shoehorned when present in minor-detail documents, 73% in no-stake controls), as expected if it is inserted regardless of context.
- Small cells: licensed groups are 39–61 documents and some "shoehorned when present" rates rest on 5–25 documents; see the Wilson intervals. The caveats in the full-run section (detection precision, lenient bias) still apply.
